F5 / R11 — WORKSPACE RELIABILITY AND TENANT ISOLATION, IN A REAL BROWSER
HEAD 6e09c28 · audit host, 2026-09-04 · against the standalone artifact behind the TLS front
$ node scripts/phase1-workspace-reliability-probe.mjs https://172.20.10.2:3051

R11 was NOT EXERCISED by the static harness: cross-tenant refusal is a runtime
property, and no test in this repository renders a Workspace route (vitest runs
environment: "node"). This run exercises it end to end — two real accounts
registered through the real form, in one real browser, against one real server.

── run result ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
30/31 checks passed.  The one failure is a PROBE DEFECT; see below.

The rows that carry R11:
   PASS  13: signing out of the first account succeeds — 200
   PASS  13: another account gets the same controlled not-found, never the
             Workspace — status 404 — "This Workspace is not available"
   PASS  13: and the outsider's own picker does not advertise it either
             — /api/workspaces returned only cmtm6wkgr00cquo77wbku91p4

and the rows that make those non-vacuous:
   PASS  3:  creating a Workspace navigates to that Workspace's own route
             — /workspaces/cmtm6vbvh00c1uo777pdbj4ud?organizationId=cmtm6v4dq00bvuo773fp0e6ec
   PASS  3:  the Workspace shell rendered, with the Workspace's name
   PASS  3:  NOT the generic server-error page — the original defect's outcome
   PASS  12: a nonexistent id renders the controlled not-found page with a 404
   PASS  12: a malformed id renders the controlled not-found page with a 404
   PASS  12: neither leaks an identifier or a stack
   PASS  10: two simultaneous submits create exactly one Workspace
             — {"statuses":[201,409],"created":1}
   PASS  11: archive removes it from the picker; restore returns it
   PASS  no document response in the whole walk was a 5xx

So the outsider is refused a Workspace that demonstrably existed and
demonstrably rendered for its owner seconds earlier. That is the isolation
claim, and it cannot pass by nothing having been created.

── the first run of this probe failed ~18 rows. Cause: the probe, not the product
Every row after step 1 reported chrome-error://chromewebdata/ and "Your
connection is not private". The audit origin is a self-signed TLS front, and this
probe launched Chrome without --ignore-certificate-errors — the flag
scripts/lib/probe-browser.mjs:136 already passes for insecure: true. Row 1a
passed alone because it is a node fetch under NODE_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED=0.
Fixed in the probe (one argv line, commented); rerun gave the 30/31 above.
Classified PROBE DEFECT.

── the one remaining FAIL, and why it is also the probe ────────────────
   FAIL  observability: the refusals produced structured access-denied lines
         — 0 lines

It counts lines in the BROWSER console. The probe's own comment says why that
ever worked: "next dev replays server-side console output into the browser
console". This run drove a production standalone build, which replays nothing —
so the count is structurally 0 there no matter how the product behaves.

The server's own log, same run, same window:
$ grep -c 'workspace.access.denied' /tmp/audit-final-server.log   → 4
$ grep -cE '@example|password|cookie|token' <those lines>          → 0
  {"level":"warn","msg":"workspace.access.denied","ts":"2026-09-04T00:02:21.840Z",
   "operation":"WorkspaceService.get","category":"WORKSPACE_NOT_FOUND",
   "stage":"workspace_unavailable","actorId":"cmtm6v4do00buuo77a4c0dwte",
   "organizationId":"…","workspaceId":"ckzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzz",
   "workspaceExists":false,"membershipExists":false}
Ids only — no email, no password, no cookie, no token. R18 holds.

── then the log itself was checked, and it held a real gap ─────────────
Those 4 lines are steps 12's two ids and two earlier runs. Step 13 — the actual
cross-tenant navigation — produced NO line. Rather than reason about it, each
refusal shape was replayed one at a time against the running artifact, waiting
2s after each so nothing was missed to buffering:

  request (outsider session, first account's workspace id)   http  new log lines
  --------------------------------------------------------   ----  -------------
  PAGE /workspaces/<id>?organizationId=<FIRST ACCOUNT'S org>  404        0
  PAGE /workspaces/<id>            (no organizationId)        404        1
  API  /api/workspaces/<id>                                   404        1
  PAGE /workspaces/<id>            (no session at all)        307        0

All four answers are correct: the 404s render "This Workspace is not available"
with 0 occurrences of the other tenant's Workspace name, and an anonymous
visitor is redirected to login rather than told the Workspace exists.

But the first row — the URL shape the probe used, and the shape a deliberate
cross-tenant probe has, because it NAMES the other tenant's organization —
was the one refusal that logged nothing. Cause, in the source:

  src/application/services/workspacePageData.ts (before the fix)
    const org = organizationId ? orgs.find(item => item.id === organizationId) : orgs[0];
    if (!org) notFound();

The organization guard runs BEFORE any Workspace lookup, so
WorkspaceService.get — the one place that logs a refusal — is never reached.
Correct answer, invisible refusal.

── the fix ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Both page-level org refusals now emit the same structured line, ids only:

  workspace.access.denied  operation=workspacePageActor
    category=ORGANIZATION_NOT_FOUND      stage=organization_unavailable
    category=ORGANIZATION_ROLE_MISSING   stage=role_unavailable
    actorId=<cuid>  organizationId=<the id the URL named, or null>

An anonymous visitor is deliberately NOT logged: that path is a redirect to
login, and a line there would be written for every crawler that finds a
Workspace URL.

Covered by src/application/services/workspacePageData.test.ts — 5 tests, the
container's real resolution path with fakes registered for the three tokens the
function resolves. Not a source scan: the assertions call the function and read
the logger.

  $ npx vitest run src/application/services/workspacePageData.test.ts
    Tests  5 passed (5)
  $ npx tsc --noEmit                                        exit=0
  $ npx eslint <the two files>                               exit=0

MUTATION (both denyOrganization calls put back to the pre-fix `notFound()`):
    Tests  3 failed | 2 passed (5)
  × refuses a URL naming another tenant's organization, and says so in the log
  × logs the refusal with ids only — no email, name, cookie or token
  × logs the separate case of a membership that no longer carries a role
  ✓ resolves the actor's own organization without logging a refusal
  ✓ sends an unauthenticated visitor to the login page instead
REVERTED → Tests 5 passed (5).

The two that stay green under the mutation are the two that must: they assert
the paths where no refusal happens. A third assertion initially stayed green
under the mutation too — JSON.stringify({}) contains no email either — so it now
asserts the line exists before asserting what is absent from it.

